WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.570
- I met Gary at some
conferences, I saw you speaking

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.423
and I was just, I thought
this man was a genius.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.500
And, not just the content that
was coming out of his mouth,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.830
which was so helpful to
a lot of these stalwarts

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.950
that were in this room that
just were very clueless

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.320
and were five years, ten years behind

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.880
when it came to what their
companies needed to do

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.170
in terms of social media.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.510
But also his energy, and
he has this thing where,

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.520
no matter what he says, you listen.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.890
Not only do you listen,
you feel like oh my god,

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:32.540
now I have to do it.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.800
I have to execute on what he's saying.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:35.753
So, come on up.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:38.655
And this is his second year doing this.

00:00:38.655 --> 00:00:41.822
(audience applauding)

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.617
- I got it.

00:00:45.637 --> 00:00:49.360
- We have a desk, wow, it's very newsy.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.020
- Hello class, how are you?

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.020
I'm doin' a lot of things,
but it's really interesting.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:57.580
I'm doin' a lot a lot of things that,

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.450
there's 50 other things I
could tell you I'm up to,

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.450
but it's really
interesting, and it's really

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.680
what we're talking about
here in it's most basic form.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.520
I'm actually only doing one thing.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.053
I'm day trading attention.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.250
I'm doing one very simple thing.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:14.100
I think the world is
in change, and I think

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.050
that there's overpriced
and under-priced attention.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.270
We're spending a lot of time here,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.040
I remember from last
year, I'd been listening,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.020
even though I was on my phone
for the first 30 minutes,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.370
here I think there's a lot of,

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.620
a lot of conversation
here around the content.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.860
You know, meaning, how do
I want to present myself?

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:29.910
A lot of things you've been debating

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.530
and asking questions about?

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.180
I think that it's a
really interesting thing

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.640
to make something happen, to
make everything that you want

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.140
to happen, it takes
content and distribution.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.930
And there's very few people
that are doing a good job

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:47.030
and really understanding
both of them at the same time

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:50.420
and more importantly, unlike
the world we grew up in,

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:53.760
where distribution was so
stagnant and understood,

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.160
the content didn't factor
distribution into it.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.920
For example, making a 22 minute sitcom

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.140
doesn't take into account
television distribution

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.430
'cause it didn't have to.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:09.530
Whereas making a video for
Instagram or Facebook or YouTube

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:12.160
has to understand how distribution works

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.100
to have a chance to be successful.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.190
So artists are struggling with math,

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.560
and mathematicians are
struggling with art.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.440
- So you said there's,
who's doing it good?

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.150
- I'm not sure, and that's the truth,

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.750
because I don't really
spend any time on auditing

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.230
who's doing it well or doing
it good or doing it smart.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.730
I spend 100% of my time on
the end consumer's attention.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.480
- So I wanna talk about
attention when it comes to you.

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.110
You have built this business
that continues to grow.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.963
I mean, last year, it was
double the size or something.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.530
But you also have a personal brand.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.000
A huge personal brand.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.880
A personal brand that a
lot of people know you

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.770
as this kind of celebrity CEO

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.380
and don't even really
understand your businesses

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.807
but understand you.
- That's right.

00:02:58.807 --> 00:03:00.040
- And the value that you're bringing

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.110
to their lives.
- That's right.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.070
- So tell me about the
decision to make yourself

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.000
a personal brand and why.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.500
- YouTube was two months old.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.880
I saw it, I'm like oh shit,

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.670
this is gonna be the next big thing,

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.880
and I started a wine
show in February 2006.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.700
I sat at a desk like this and
drank four bottles of wine

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.823
for 20 minutes and it exploded.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:24.700
Google, Yahoo, because Yahoo
video was big at the time,

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.210
Yahoo and Google, Google
had bought YouTube

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.043
when they flew me out.

00:03:28.043 --> 00:03:31.310
But very early on, I had
20 minute shows on YouTube.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:32.520
Like if you go back to YouTube

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.870
and search videos that were made in 2006,

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:38.100
nobody was making 20
minute videos like I was.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.680
I was just long winded and talked a lot

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.680
and so they dragged out,
but I built a personal brand

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.310
very early on in the scheme of things

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.010
because of the YouTube show,
and then Twitter came out

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.280
and that's when I started getting smart

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.410
and started investing in these things.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.490
I'm like oh, that's gonna
be the next big thing.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:57.830
So I got on Twitter early, I
was one of the first 25 people

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.870
in the world to have a
million followers on Twitter,

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.610
which is really wild
because everybody else

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:04.470
was from Tech TV and,
you know, Digg Nation,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.350
and it was very techy,
Twitter the first two years.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.293
I was a wine retailer in New Jersey.

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.730
- What did you think--
- So I built a brand early.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:15.820
- What did you think using
your face, your name,

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.183
your likeness, meant to the business?

00:04:18.183 --> 00:04:20.270
Was it just about being
famous or was it about--

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:24.040
- No. I thought I was gonna do QVC

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.600
the first day I did Wine Library TV.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.260
By the time my stock guy
who set up the camera

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:34.243
was set up and we were
ready to film, I got scared.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.440
A friend, I recalled this very recently,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.370
I did something very early on in my career

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.230
where it was extremely noble for,

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.930
pretty radical reasons
that I didn't even need to

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.580
or should have even maybe, right?

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.317
And the kid asked me why did I do that?

00:04:51.317 --> 00:04:54.223
And my answer was, because I'm scared

00:04:54.223 --> 00:04:57.363
that Dateline's gonna roll up
on me and ask me the question.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.260
Intuitively, at it's earliest point,

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.630
I understood the internet was gonna expose

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:06.490
all the shadows of our society.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:09.460
Like, at a very young
age, I figured it out.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.000
I didn't realize it, but
all my actions spoke to it.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.100
So when Wine Library TV's
first episode started,

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.140
even though I wanted to sell wine,

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.290
I was like shit, what if I'm at a party,

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:19.610
and somebody gives me a glass of wine

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:20.810
and says, if Ryan's like hey,

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:21.643
what do you think of this wine?

00:05:21.643 --> 00:05:22.476
And I'm like, oh it's terrible,

00:05:22.476 --> 00:05:23.480
you're like, well you're a piece of shit,

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.737
you said it was phenomenal on episode 136.

00:05:25.737 --> 00:05:28.190
And I was so scared of that,

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:29.570
that basically from the beginning,

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:34.440
I went ultra transparent,
but I thought I was,

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:35.980
I was doing it for business.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:40.470
I was 32 years old, I was an
entrepreneur business man,

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.140
there was no fame, there was no,

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:43.470
I was already you know, I was a grown up.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:46.430
Like I wasn't thinking, it
wasn't what I dreamed to do,

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.410
it's nothing that I, I
never thought that I would

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.270
walk the rec FO like I just did right now

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:53.103
and take nine selfies.

00:05:53.103 --> 00:05:56.340
That was never a part of the equation,

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.129
it was for business, I was a business man.

00:05:59.129 --> 00:06:00.170
- Do you think having a personal brand

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:01.730
is making you more money?

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:03.500
- A hundred percent, but
I don't think you need

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.160
a personal brand to build your business.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.190
I think you need self-awareness
to build your business.

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:12.010
And so, even listening
to some of the questions,

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:13.800
like David D's question here,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.790
like when I was listening
to some of the questions,

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:18.360
and I brought this up last year in this,

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.620
and I'm gonna say this again,

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:20.760
I understand the class you're in,

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.760
but I'm an unbelievable buyer
of betting on your strengths

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.700
and punting your weaknesses.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:31.260
And the thought of
operators and entrepreneurs

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.670
forcing themselves to
create personal brands

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:36.300
is laughable, because
nine out of ten of 'em

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.880
that I know have no shot.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.000
Or are gonna waste way too much energy,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.460
or it's super uncomfortable.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.120
Now that doesn't mean
if you're an introvert,

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.950
you know, it was funny,
when you talk about

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:47.500
somebody just standing there,

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:48.910
I've always thought about authenticity.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:51.720
Like if you are actually
monotone, if like,

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.460
for example, I think
entertainment and, you know,

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.250
I have shtick, fine.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.470
That works for me.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:59.970
But some of the stuff that I most enjoy

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:01.613
is just depth of knowledge.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.200
The people that have followed
me around personal brands,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.780
a lot of them are not
charismatic or pretty

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:11.150
or that interesting, there's
just such a deep expert

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:14.080
in their field that their podcast,

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:15.970
for example, is dominating.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:18.030
And even their video of just like them

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.980
straight at the camera,
not dressed the part,

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:23.950
not all that charismatic,
a little bit awkward,

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:25.430
but their information--
- But to me,

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:26.835
that's still a personal brand.

00:07:26.835 --> 00:07:28.960
- Well that's where I'm going,

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.110
I totally agree.
- At the beginning

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:33.550
of this course, I say that
it's not about being big

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:34.383
and it's not about being like you,

00:07:34.383 --> 00:07:36.587
but you can be shy, you can be awkward,

00:07:36.587 --> 00:07:38.500
you can be, and you can lean into that.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:39.510
- A hundred percent.
- That can be your

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:40.830
personal brand.
- And then just having context

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:43.793
of like, what I'm assuming
the ambition of this room is.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:46.750
It's about self awareness.

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:49.810
And back to my self awareness, you know,

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:51.560
as it started happening I'm like, oh yeah,

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:54.420
I was a good class clown and oh yeah,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.250
I do wanna talk at the dinner table

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:57.650
and control the conversation.

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:00.040
So like, it became obvious to me,

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:01.313
wait a minute, there's something here.

00:08:01.313 --> 00:08:04.010
And then when I made the
transition of talking

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:06.610
about business, not about wine,

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:09.340
it really went to a
totally different place

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:12.910
because, you know, this is what's great

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:15.220
about documenting everything,
I was saying things

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:18.267
that nobody agreed with and
then ended up being true.

00:08:18.267 --> 00:08:19.843
You know, like ...

00:08:21.583 --> 00:08:22.670
- Like what?

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:24.970
- Like Instagram is gonna
get bought by Facebook,

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.320
and that became true, and then
I'm on CNN for predicting it

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:28.890
and then they're making fun of me

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:31.875
because it was a billion
dollars and I said he stole it.

00:08:31.875 --> 00:08:35.260
And if you go read the comments
on YouTube of the clip,

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.840
the first two years
everybody makes fun of me:

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.900
asshole, pushing propaganda,

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:42.610
how can you pay a billion
dollars for a company

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.290
that's only 500 days old.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.340
And then the last two
years, it's like genius,

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.573
he fucking knew, you know.

00:08:48.573 --> 00:08:51.320
So what's amazing is
being historically correct

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:52.960
is the most valuable thing to do.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.770
I think one of the great reasons
to build a personal brand

00:08:55.770 --> 00:09:00.770
is to document, you know, imagine
watching the documentation

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.270
of all the things we put on a pedestal.

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:06.720
Like imagine if Steve Jobs
had a vlog back in the day.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.681
Like, the learning, for me the reason--

00:09:08.681 --> 00:09:10.710
- What percentage of your
day that you document?

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.310
'Cause I know you
document a heck of a lot.

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:14.270
- Iris is filming right now.

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:15.583
- Oh god, Iris.
- Some.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.760
- What's tough for me is I'm
operating an actual business,

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:21.740
so there's a lot of firing and negotiating

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:23.750
and a lot of stuff we don't film.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.030
I do film a lot of it,

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:26.933
just for my grandkids to watch one day--

00:09:26.933 --> 00:09:28.580
- Film somebody being fired?

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:29.890
- I haven't, I haven't.

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:33.450
I haven't done that
because I just feel bad,

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:34.283
yeah I don't think that's,

00:09:34.283 --> 00:09:37.260
but I'll film things that, or I'll ask,

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.206
or I'll film from afar.

00:09:39.206 --> 00:09:41.170
You know, I also am projecting technology.

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:43.640
One of the biggest
reasons I document my life

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:46.380
is that I think my kids
are gonna sell the IP

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:47.870
of people being able to be in meetings

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:50.410
with me in the future
and be our environment.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.920
So there's a lot of
chess that I'm playing.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.667
I do a lot of things--

00:09:57.865 --> 00:09:58.760
- I have a question.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:00.740
I'm gonna ask you about the class

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:04.149
and some of them, how
many of you are on social?

00:10:04.149 --> 00:10:05.760
How many are on?

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.060
And how many's on nothing?

00:10:09.179 --> 00:10:12.363
A few, I'm gonna be you in 15 years.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:15.490
Definitely, it is a goal of mine.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:18.420
So they're in so many different sectors,

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.570
in consulting to retail to entertainment,

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:24.630
I mean so many different sectors.

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:27.780
Talk about what each platform means

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:29.160
which should help them say, okay,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.590
I need to spend more time here.

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:32.870
So let's start with Facebook.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:34.800
- Okay, before you we
that, just to frame it up,

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:36.850
first of all we have to decide, you know,

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.040
what you're doing, meaning,

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:43.290
you know I always question
would I have even been

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:45.560
on social media if I didn't
use it for business purposes.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:47.180
Like, I don't consume content.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:49.290
You were talkin' about, you
know, some of the streaming TV,

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:51.180
like I consume very little outside

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:52.960
of the Knicks and the Jets,

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:56.218
my consumption of actual
content, because I'm reading,

00:10:56.218 --> 00:10:58.880
I'm consuming people's
behavior on the internet.

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:01.053
That's what I'm doing and consuming.

00:11:01.905 --> 00:11:04.930
So I think as individuals,
that and Adam, that's great,

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.050
like I don't think that's
weird, that's fine.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:11.000
I think a business not being
on it is ludicrous, right?

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:14.018
So, are you asking me on
a personal brand level?

00:11:14.018 --> 00:11:15.390
- Personal brand.
